WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:10.510
I'm going to ask a question and you can’t
Google it.

00:00:10.510 --> 00:00:13.420
What’s the biggest agricultural power in
Europe?

00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:17.120
Who is the biggest food exporter in all across the EU?

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:21.230
Many will think Spain, Italy, or maybe France.

00:00:21.230 --> 00:00:29.880
After all, these countries have good weather and thousands of hectares of arable land.

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:37.190
However, none of these countries gets close
to THE NETHERLANDS, also known as HOLLAND.

00:00:37.190 --> 00:00:43.120
To give you an idea, Holland is the largest
food exporter in the world, second only to

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:44.120
the United States.

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:45.120
What are you saying?

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:46.120
You’re not surprised?

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:47.120
Well think about it.

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:50.400
We’re talking about such a small country,
that they even had to build dams to gain some

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:51.510
land from the sea.

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:54.760
In fact, it’s one of the most densely populated
countries in the world.

00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:55.760
Which means, they barely have any land to
cultivate.

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:59.610
To this we need to add the fact that they
are one of the richest and most modern countries

00:00:59.610 --> 00:01:00.610
in the world.

00:01:00.610 --> 00:01:03.582
The first stock market created in history
was the Amsterdam stock exchange.

00:01:03.582 --> 00:01:11.210
And I’m sure you all know Dutch companies
such as SHELL, PHILIPS or the ING bank.

00:01:11.210 --> 00:01:17.590
Normally, when a country has many industrial
or service companies, agriculture is placed

00:01:17.590 --> 00:01:19.330
on the back burner.

00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:24.789
You may be wondering: Why?

00:01:24.789 --> 00:01:29.200
Well imagine that we have a piece of land,
for example in Spain, that measures one hectare.

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:34.020
If we use it to plant potatoes, we can earn
between 7,000 and 8,000 euros per hectare.

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:38.450
Now, imagine the amount of money that we could
earn if we used that area to build a factory

00:01:38.450 --> 00:01:39.759
or an office building.

00:01:39.759 --> 00:01:43.040
Do you understand now?

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:47.729
Well, despite all this, Holland is the world
leader in potato exports.

00:01:47.729 --> 00:01:53.060
And is above France, Spain or even the United
States.

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:58.630
I know what many of you may be thinking.

00:01:58.630 --> 00:01:59.630
Agriculture in Europe?

00:01:59.630 --> 00:02:02.450
I’m sure Brussels is giving out some grants
somewhere!

00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:07.980
Agriculture is one of the most protected sectors
with the most aid in the entire European Union.

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:11.480
However, the Netherlands isn’t the country
that receives the most money.

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:14.440
Of course, they receive aid like every other
member country.

00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:18.170
But they don’t get even a third of what
Greece gets, for example.

00:02:18.170 --> 00:02:23.370
And if we compare it with larger countries
like France, the differences are abysmal.

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:28.650
So how is it possible that with so little
land and so little help, Holland has become

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:31.180
the market garden of the world?

00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:41.040
As you can imagine, the answer is simply defined
in one word: TECHNOLOGY.

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:45.450
In a farm in the Netherlands we can find tractors
that drive themselves, drones that monitor

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:49.450
crops and, of course, resource-optimization
at the highest level.

00:02:49.450 --> 00:02:54.220
However, there’s still a question: Why does
Holland have a more modern agriculture than,

00:02:54.220 --> 00:02:55.580
for example, Spain, which has more aid from
Europe?

00:02:55.580 --> 00:02:59.629
What has the Netherlands done that can be
imitated in other countries?

00:02:59.629 --> 00:03:07.739
Today we’re going to answer these questions
but before, let’s take a look back at history.

00:03:07.739 --> 00:03:09.540
FROM THE FIELD TO THE UNIVERSITY

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:14.030
In the 70s, Holland was one of the most prosperous
economies in Europe.

00:03:14.030 --> 00:03:17.890
Companies such as Philips or Airbus were conquering
international markets.

00:03:17.890 --> 00:03:21.180
And all this were good news.

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:26.260
But, as we always say in VISUALPOLITIK, not
everything is a field of roses.

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:30.260
Imagine being 20 and living in the Netherlands
back at in time.

00:03:30.260 --> 00:03:31.560
Where would you have liked to work?

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:35.250
In an international company like Philips,
with high salaries and good conditions...

00:03:35.250 --> 00:03:37.910
or on your father's farm?

00:03:37.910 --> 00:03:38.910
Exactly!

00:03:38.910 --> 00:03:41.980
Dutch agriculture was on the verge of disappearing.

00:03:41.980 --> 00:03:44.650
We have to add something very important to
this.

00:03:44.650 --> 00:03:49.140
As we said before, the Netherlands is very
small and there’s hardly any space for large

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:53.890
farms.

00:03:53.890 --> 00:03:56.150
For example, there’s plenty of land in the
United States.

00:03:56.150 --> 00:03:59.540
And that means that farmers can group and
create large farms.

00:03:59.540 --> 00:04:03.030
In fact, nowadays a lot of American farms
belong to large companies.

00:04:03.030 --> 00:04:08.290
This means that they can make scale investments
and, for example, buy planes to fumigate huge

00:04:08.290 --> 00:04:09.290
areas of land.

00:04:09.290 --> 00:04:11.959
However, in the case of the Netherlands, farms
are small.

00:04:11.959 --> 00:04:17.400
With an average size of around 7 hectares,
making large investments became really complicated.

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:22.570
To make matters worse, by the 70s, Dutch salaries
were already much higher than in other European

00:04:22.570 --> 00:04:23.570
countries.

00:04:23.570 --> 00:04:27.540
In other words, unlike the farmers in Southern
Italy, the Dutch couldn’t compete with low

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:28.540
prices.

00:04:28.540 --> 00:04:35.850
And you may be wondering, why did they need
agriculture?

00:04:35.850 --> 00:04:40.280
After all, in those years, there was already
a certain degree of international trades.

00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:45.230
The Netherlands could import food while they
manufactured televisions or airplanes, right?

00:04:45.230 --> 00:04:47.169
Yes, you’re right.

00:04:47.169 --> 00:04:51.520
From a purely economic point of view, they
could’ve easily let go of their primary

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:52.520
sector.

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:55.910
But, my dear friends of VISUALPOLITIK, not
everything in life is economy.

00:04:55.910 --> 00:04:58.570
And in a country like Holland, peasant associations
have a lot of power.

00:04:58.570 --> 00:05:01.080
More than in almost any other country.

00:05:01.080 --> 00:05:04.940
For example, in many other countries they
have something called the Social Dialogue

00:05:04.940 --> 00:05:08.980
table, which is an organization where the
government, employers and unions meet.

00:05:08.980 --> 00:05:10.580
Well, in the Netherlands they have something
similar.

00:05:10.580 --> 00:05:16.949
But along with employers and unions, farmers
and rancher associations are also well represented.

00:05:16.949 --> 00:05:23.230
And in the 70s, Dutch farmers and ranchers
weren’t willing to change jobs.

00:05:23.230 --> 00:05:28.270
So, put yourself in this situation: Holland
had to maintain its primary sector at all

00:05:28.270 --> 00:05:29.270
costs.

00:05:29.270 --> 00:05:31.870
But, at the same time, all the odds were against
them.

00:05:31.870 --> 00:05:34.500
At this point, I know what you may be thinking...

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:35.500
Grants!

00:05:35.500 --> 00:05:39.570
The solution would be to give subsidies to
the farmers so they’d stop protesting!

00:05:39.570 --> 00:05:40.680
Right?

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:47.830
Of course, it wouldn’t be the first nor
the last time such policies are applied.

00:05:47.830 --> 00:05:50.300
But the Dutch government had other plans.

00:05:50.300 --> 00:05:54.330
Now that they had to maintain their primary
sector, they wanted to do it well.

00:05:54.330 --> 00:05:59.139
And, since they couldn’t reduce salaries
or group farms, there was only one solution

00:05:59.139 --> 00:06:01.449
left: to improve productivity.

00:06:01.449 --> 00:06:06.180
That is, getting the farmers to produce more
and better while working.

00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:07.180
How?

00:06:07.180 --> 00:06:12.919
We’ll see that right now!

00:06:12.919 --> 00:06:14.880
THE UNIVERSITY OF WAGENINGEN

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:20.490
You’ve surely heard it a thousand times:
Silicon Valley wouldn’t be what it is today

00:06:20.490 --> 00:06:22.710
if it weren’t for Stanford University.

00:06:22.710 --> 00:06:27.790
It’s the mecca of research in everything
that has to do with electronics and telecommunications.

00:06:27.790 --> 00:06:30.180
But they don’t only research!

00:06:30.180 --> 00:06:33.100
They also train the best computer scientists
in the world.

00:06:33.100 --> 00:06:39.009
And as they leave the university, they go
directly to big companies like GOOGLE, APPLE

00:06:39.009 --> 00:06:40.009
or TESLA.

00:06:40.009 --> 00:06:44.570
Well, as far as agriculture is concerned,
the mecca of innovation isn’t in the US

00:06:44.570 --> 00:06:45.980
but in the Netherlands.

00:06:45.980 --> 00:06:53.430
Yes, friends of VISUALPOLITIK, I’m talking
about the University of Wageningen, also known

00:06:53.430 --> 00:06:54.710
as FOOD VALLEY.

00:06:54.710 --> 00:06:57.139
Many of you might not have heard about this
university.

00:06:57.139 --> 00:07:00.910
That’s because they only work in the world
of agriculture.

00:07:00.910 --> 00:07:05.570
They don’t have degrees in law or art history.

00:07:05.570 --> 00:07:09.180
Wageningen's motto is to improve food quality
in the entire world.

00:07:09.180 --> 00:07:12.820
You may be wondering, but what is so special
about this University?

00:07:12.820 --> 00:07:14.710
The first difference is money.

00:07:14.710 --> 00:07:18.300
The Netherlands knew that it needed technology
to improve its fields.

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:19.930
So what did they do?

00:07:19.930 --> 00:07:22.550
They covered their scientists with money.

00:07:22.550 --> 00:07:25.949
In just two decades, Wageningen University’s
research budget doubled.

00:07:25.949 --> 00:07:30.639
But wait a moment because money isn’t the
only important thing!

00:07:30.639 --> 00:07:38.419
Truth is, the most important thing wasn’t
HOW MUCH but WHERE that money was coming from.

00:07:38.419 --> 00:07:44.850
See... in many European countries and in most
of Latin America, there’s a very clear line

00:07:44.850 --> 00:07:47.990
that separates the public and private universities.

00:07:47.990 --> 00:07:50.410
Public universities live from the State.

00:07:50.410 --> 00:07:51.410
Period.

00:07:51.410 --> 00:07:53.800
Of course they can have some kind of collaboration
with private companies.

00:07:53.800 --> 00:07:56.400
But... let's be honest, this barely represents
a small percentage of their entire funding.

00:07:56.400 --> 00:07:57.979
Wageningen’s case is different.

00:07:57.979 --> 00:08:02.780
50% of their money comes from the Government,
25% from the university's own income and the

00:08:02.780 --> 00:08:05.000
last 25% from private companies.

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:07.039
And I know what you’re thinking!

00:08:07.039 --> 00:08:09.069
Private companies financing a university?

00:08:09.069 --> 00:08:11.370
Does this mean that the university is private?

00:08:11.370 --> 00:08:12.370
Of course not!

00:08:12.370 --> 00:08:13.900
But it is a great advantage.

00:08:13.900 --> 00:08:18.009
It allows the university to be connected to
the rest of society.

00:08:18.009 --> 00:08:19.009
Ok!

00:08:19.009 --> 00:08:20.009
Ok!

00:08:20.009 --> 00:08:24.720
Sorry, I know that sounded like a politician’s
bluff.

00:08:24.720 --> 00:08:25.720
I’m sorry.

00:08:25.720 --> 00:08:26.720
I'll explain with an example.

00:08:26.720 --> 00:08:29.389
While preparing this video, we telephoned
CESAR BRAVO, a farmer from Spain, more specifically

00:08:29.389 --> 00:08:33.810
from the Canary Islands, to ask about how
agricultural modernization works.

00:08:33.810 --> 00:08:38.310
You may not know this, but livestock is very
important in the Canary Islands.

00:08:38.310 --> 00:08:39.370
Especially goats.

00:08:39.370 --> 00:08:44.410
They have a specific type of goat, called
PALMER GOAT, which is unique in the world.

00:08:44.410 --> 00:08:48.660
And, logically, there are several institutes
and foundations that dedicate to studying

00:08:48.660 --> 00:08:50.540
this type of goat.

00:08:50.540 --> 00:09:00.350
However, as CESAR explained, practically none
of the studies they make are useful to farmers.

00:09:00.350 --> 00:09:01.350
Think about it!

00:09:01.350 --> 00:09:03.600
If you have a goat farm, what kind of research
do you need?

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:08.120
You’ll want to know how to produce more
and better milk, right?

00:09:08.120 --> 00:09:12.670
Studies on how to better feed the animals,
dietary supplements or even genetic engineering.

00:09:12.670 --> 00:09:18.350
Well, if you go to the ICIA website, for example,
which is the Canary Institute of Agricultural

00:09:18.350 --> 00:09:20.930
Research, you won’t find anything like that.

00:09:20.930 --> 00:09:24.900
There are tons of academic articles that explain
the racial origin of the palm goat, whether

00:09:24.900 --> 00:09:29.910
it’s related to other goats in Morocco or
Tanzania or if they existed during the middles

00:09:29.910 --> 00:09:30.910
ages.

00:09:30.910 --> 00:09:31.990
Practical use?

00:09:31.990 --> 00:09:34.480
Practically zero.

00:09:34.480 --> 00:09:41.050
Meanwhile, according to the TIMES HIGHER EDUCATION
ranking, Wageningen’s studies are the most

00:09:41.050 --> 00:09:42.620
cited in their field.

00:09:42.620 --> 00:09:43.620
But that’s not all!

00:09:43.620 --> 00:09:47.089
I bet you can’t guess what the university’s
official language is.

00:09:47.089 --> 00:09:50.990
You surely think that by being in Holland,
they’d speak Dutch, right?

00:09:50.990 --> 00:09:52.649
Well, none of that!

00:09:52.649 --> 00:09:56.390
We’re talking about the world capital of
agricultural research!

00:09:56.390 --> 00:09:57.390
But not only that!

00:09:57.390 --> 00:09:58.390
Record numbers of foreign students at Dutch
universities of technology

00:09:58.390 --> 00:09:59.432
In this university, more than 40% of all students
are foreign.

00:09:59.432 --> 00:10:00.770
As is the research staff.

00:10:00.770 --> 00:10:04.910
No matter where you’re from, if you have
a talent for agriculture, you can get a place

00:10:04.910 --> 00:10:08.630
in Wageningen.

00:10:08.630 --> 00:10:16.600
Which is why the official language is English.

00:10:16.600 --> 00:10:21.840
That explains why Wageningen is, for example,
the pioneer in LED lamps for greenhouses,

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:26.370
allowing to create precisely controlled artificial
microclimates.

00:10:26.370 --> 00:10:29.779
Thanks to this, the Netherlands is one of
the leaders in tomato growth, even though

00:10:29.779 --> 00:10:32.910
in natural conditions, tomatoes would never
grow in such a cold country.

00:10:32.910 --> 00:10:33.910
But not only that!

00:10:33.910 --> 00:10:40.000
In a country like the US, farmers need about
126 liters of water to grow 1 kilogram of

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:41.000
tomatoes.

00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:44.930
Well, thanks to this university’s research,
now one can produce the same amount with just

00:10:44.930 --> 00:10:46.500
8 liters of water.

00:10:46.500 --> 00:10:51.440
Imagine how much saving that means both from
an economic and ecological perspective.

00:10:51.440 --> 00:10:53.470
But wait a moment!

00:10:53.470 --> 00:10:58.060
Research is good but… it really isn’t
worth anything if it stays within the university.

00:10:58.060 --> 00:11:05.310
And in that, Holland is again an example of
how to do things right.

00:11:05.310 --> 00:11:10.470
FROM THE UNIVERSITY TO THE FIELD

00:11:10.470 --> 00:11:12.470
Let’s get back to Spain for a moment.

00:11:12.470 --> 00:11:17.020
Imagine that we have a tomato garden and want
to invest in this hydroponic technology that

00:11:17.020 --> 00:11:18.420
will allow us to save on water.

00:11:18.420 --> 00:11:23.209
The European Union, more precisely the Common
Agricultural Policy, provides all kinds of

00:11:23.209 --> 00:11:26.029
grants so that farmers can modernize their
fields.

00:11:26.029 --> 00:11:30.380
So we’ll go to the Agricultural Extension
Agency, which is where all these grants are

00:11:30.380 --> 00:11:31.380
managed.

00:11:31.380 --> 00:11:34.240
Well, we’ll get our money there and they’ll
make sure that we will actually spend it to

00:11:34.240 --> 00:11:36.160
improve our production techniques.

00:11:36.160 --> 00:11:38.279
So far so good.

00:11:38.279 --> 00:11:44.200
But, how do we know what machines to hire?

00:11:44.200 --> 00:11:47.650
One would think that the farmer would know,
better than anyone, what their lands would

00:11:47.650 --> 00:11:48.650
need.

00:11:48.650 --> 00:11:52.640
But the truth is that the agricultural world
evolves as quickly as Internet companies.

00:11:52.640 --> 00:11:56.390
And if nobody advises us, we’ll have to
find the information ourselves.

00:11:56.390 --> 00:12:00.710
Well, that’s the situation in which almost
every Spanish farmer is!

00:12:00.710 --> 00:12:04.550
There’s a huge gap between the university
and the field.

00:12:04.550 --> 00:12:07.170
The Dutch case is entirely different.

00:12:07.170 --> 00:12:11.470
In this country they have the so-called Agricultural
Council Offices.

00:12:11.470 --> 00:12:15.339
That means that there’s a person in charge
of explaining what type of machines we should

00:12:15.339 --> 00:12:20.350
buy and what technologies are best suited
for our type of crop.

00:12:20.350 --> 00:12:23.350
The result?

00:12:23.350 --> 00:12:28.600
The Dutch field looks nothing like those of
any other country.

00:12:28.600 --> 00:12:32.550
While most of the European countries consume
tons of antibiotics for animals, the Netherland’s

00:12:32.550 --> 00:12:36.860
ranchers are already using the most advanced
veterinarian techniques in such a way that

00:12:36.860 --> 00:12:39.480
they can prevent diseases instead of curing
them.

00:12:39.480 --> 00:12:42.100
The same happens with plant pesticides.

00:12:42.100 --> 00:12:45.400
In this country they noticed that if they
use drones to monitor the crops, they can

00:12:45.400 --> 00:12:50.350
control each and every one of the vegetables,
and prevent them from becoming infected.

00:12:50.350 --> 00:12:54.220
And in a country where almost half of the
crops are irrigated, they have managed to

00:12:54.220 --> 00:12:57.790
decrease their water consumption by almost
90%.

00:12:57.790 --> 00:13:02.970
And agricultural product exports surpassed
92 billion euros last year.

00:13:02.970 --> 00:13:04.130
And not only that!

00:13:04.130 --> 00:13:11.000
Holland is also making money by exporting
their own technology.

00:13:11.000 --> 00:13:20.780
The UAE goes Dutch to ensure their food security
So the question is, do you think other countries

00:13:20.780 --> 00:13:22.980
can import these kinds of policies?

00:13:22.980 --> 00:13:27.649
How far can the technologies developed in
Holland help feed the entire world?

00:13:27.649 --> 00:13:29.149
Leave your answer in the comments.

00:13:29.149 --> 00:13:35.050
Before we finish, I want to thank CESAR BRAVO
and ANGEL GAGO, from AGQ Nutrition, for helping

00:13:35.050 --> 00:13:36.050
me in this video.

00:13:36.050 --> 00:13:39.920
And if you’re interested in this subject,
we’ll soon release another video explaining

00:13:39.920 --> 00:13:43.139
the European Agricultural Policy, the so-called
CAP, in depth.

